1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="text-indent: 1em"&gt;E. p. 156.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;Sept. 19,1659. &lt;em&gt;The declaration of Jane, the Indian, of Scarboro&amp;#39;, concerning Land. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This aforesai
2 CONC d Jane, alias Uphannum, doth declare that her mother, namely, Nagaasqua, wife of Wickwarrawaske, Sagamore, and ber brother, viz. &lt;span&gt;Ugagoquskit, &lt;/span&gt;and herself, viz. Uphannum, coequally have sold unto &lt;span&gt;Andrew Alger &lt
2 CONC ;/span&gt;and his hrother Arthur Alger, a tract of land beginning at the mouth of the river called Blue Point River, where the river doth part, and so up along with the river called Owascoage in Indian and so up 3 score poles above the falls on th
2 CONC e one side, and on the other side bounded up along with the northernmost river that dreaneth by the great hill of Abram Jocelyn andgoeth northward bounding from the head of this River S. VV. and so to the aforesaid bounds, viz. 60 poles above the F
2 CONC alls : This aforesaid Uphannum doth declare that her mother and brother and she have already in hand ,&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;received full satisfaction of the aforesaid Algers, for the aforesaid their &lt;em&gt;S &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;land fr
2 CONC om the beginning of the world to this day, provided that from time to time, from year to year yearly, the aforesaid Algers shall peaceably suffer Uphannum to plant in Andrew Alger&amp;#39;s field so long as Uphannum and her mother Nagaasqua both li
2 CONC ve, and also one bushel of corn for acknowledgement every year so long as they both shall live. Uphannum doth declare that the bargain was made in the year 1651, unto which she doth subscribe. In the presence of Cook, the day and date abovewritten
2 CONC , Jane an Indian woman did appear before &lt;span&gt;me &lt;/span&gt;21 June, 1672, and acknowledge this instrument was the deed of her mother and herself. Before &lt;span&gt;me, &lt;/span&gt;B. PENDLETON, Associate.&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;An
2 CONC drew Augur left in all six children, three sons and three daughters, but the property chiefly descended to the heirs of John, the oldest&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
