2 TITL Jonas and Mary&#39;s Story
1 _OID be6ef33f-8669-414d-82b4-a77c512c6117
1 PLAC Groton, Mass.
1 _DSCR Take from &quot;The Prescott Memorial,&quot; page 43.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: x-small; color: #000000;"&gt;Butler, p. 287. John Loker, of whom we have no other account than 89 connected with this affair, is said to hav
2 CONC e been wealthy, and both he and his wife to have been somewhat aristocratic in their feelings and notions. Having only one daughter, and she exceedingly fair and of good promise, they disdained to betroth her to a blacksmith, the son of a blacksmit
2 CONC h, however rich or otherwise unexceptionable he might be. They had set their hearts on Mary's marrying a lawyer. So when they found that there was a strong attachment between their idol, Mary, and the young blacksmith (Jonas Prescott), they remonst
2 CONC rated, but, like many other imprudent parents, they unwittingly pursued a course well calculated to foster and strengthen it. They forbade his entering their house, or having any communication whatever with their daughter; and the more effectuall
2 CONC y to prevent any intercourse, they grated the windows of her apartment, in the house; and when they thought there was any danger of an interview between them, they locked her in. Jonas and Mary however were not to be baffled by grates and locks. Jo
2 CONC nas took opportunities, when the cold night wind blew and the pelting storm raged, when no listener could overhear their soft whisperings, to place himself beneath her grated window and there enjoy sweet communion with his beloved Mary. Their inter
2 CONC course was soon discovered, however, by the vigilant and chagrineil parents. The next expedient resorted to was to place her in some secluded spot under the care of some watchful and faithful guardian. Chocksett, now called Sterling, then in fronti
2 CONC er settlement, although adjoining to Groton, was chosen as the place of her seclusion. Jonas searched the country around, and made diligent inquiry to find the place of her banishment, for some time in vain. At length, being one day in the wilds o
2 CONC f Chocksett, he made his usual inquiry of some young men he saw if they had any pretty girls in their neighborhood. They told him there was to be a quilting that very day, where all their girls would be in; that they were going in the evening to da
2 CONC nce with them and invited him to accompany them, where he might see for himself. He very cheer- fnllv accepted the invitation, and on arriving at the cottage where the seamstresses of the settlement were assembled, whom should he there find but hi
2 CONC s beloved Mary Loker. This was indeed to them a happy adventure. Concealing, as well as they could, their former acquaintance, they took opportunities to be partners m the dance and made assignments for future meetings. Having thus fortunately disc
2 CONC overed the place of banishment, he renewed his visits, till her parents, finding it out, to&amp;laquo;k her home. She was then sternly told, that she must reject the blacksmith and receive the addresses of the lawyer. She resolutely replied, " Sh
2 CONC e would never marry to any one but Jonas Prescott." The rejoinder was, " Then you shall never have a farthing of our property." To this there was a general demurrer; a decree for marriage without dowry followed. The consummation took place before e
2 CONC ven the most common utensils for housekeeping could be procured (perhaps there was some delay to see if the old folks would not relent and procure or provide some). The tradition asserts that her only implements for boiling was a two quart kettle
2 CONC , and her wash tub the shell of a large pumpkin. From this affectionate and happy pair sprung the doctors, warriors, civilians, statesmen, jurists, historians, &amp;amp;c., noticed in this genealogical record and memoir, with numerous other descend
2 CONC ants of whom Mary lived to see one hundred and seventy-five. She d. Oct. 28, 1735, aged pre cisely eighty-two years and one month. &amp;mdash; See Butler's History of Oroton, pp. 287-8.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
