2 CONC 25, Jacob van Steenbergen, b. at Kingston, grandson of the emigrant Jan Janszen, from Amersfoort, later called "van Steenbergen,&amp;rdquo; by trade "timmerman,&amp;rdquo; or carpenter, who 15 May, 1657, as a batchelor and freeman was employed fo
2 CONC r 6 months as city carpenter at New York. Jan's marriage, 3 Oct., 1660, is the first recorded in the Kingston Church Records, and he was one of those who signed the original Kingston Compact to live together and erect a stockade, in 1658. Otherwis
2 CONC e the Kingston Court Records show he was a rough and tough ancestor. His wife, Catryntje Matthysen, i.e. Catharine, dau. of Mathew, was dau. of Mattys Janszen (i.e.Mathew,son of John),who d. at Kingston before Feb., 1663, and whose widow Margarit
2 CONC a Hendricksen, m. Capt.Thomas Chambers, patriotic English settler, so prominent in early Kingston, after whom Jan's eldest son Thomas van Steenbergen (bap.1 July, 1663) was named. Margarietde Duytser&amp;rsquo;s husband Jacob van Steenbergen, was e
2 CONC ither the son of this Thomas, or of his brother Mattheisvan Steenbergen, bap. 31March,1678, who 12 March,1699, m. Marritje Theuniszen, i.e.Mary, dau. of Anthony, b. at Kingston, dau. of Theunis Elysse (i.e,son of Elias)van Bunschoten. The sponsor
2 CONC s or godparents indicate that Mattheis was the&amp;nbsp; father of&amp;nbsp; Jacob,&amp;nbsp; but no&amp;nbsp; baptismal entry is of&amp;nbsp; record. If Jacob&amp;rsquo;s father was Thomas van Steenbergen, then the mother was Maria Salome Adamsze
2 CONC n &amp;ldquo;Metzelaer," i.e. Maria Salome, dau. of Adam, the stone mason, whom Thomasm.16Feb.,1683, and who was bap.14Jan.,1665, dau. of Jan Adamszen &amp;ldquo;Metzelaer" and Geertie Dircks, his wife. Jan Adamszen Metzelaer was involved in a disa
2 CONC greeable prosecution for slander of one of the prominent Dutch dames, in a manner not unknown among the early settlers&amp;mdash; the result of a loose tongue excited by tap room brandy; but stoutly denied the charge, and was threatened and &amp;ld
2 CONC quo;prepared for torture". The appearance of Hermanus Reynders and his wife as sponsors to Elizabeth van Steenbergen, is another of the suggestions of relationship between Roel of Willemszen&amp;rsquo;s dau. Mageltje and the other Dutchers.&lt;/spa
