2 CONC p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;!--StartFragment--&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: center;" align="center"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;EDWARD WINN (abt
2 CONC . 1604-1682) and JOANNA (unknown) (abt. 1607-1649)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: center;" align="center"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;My 9G Grandparents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p
2 CONC &gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: center;" align="center"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;By Betty Gillespie Pollack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: center;" align="center"&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;Copyright December 2018 - Permission to reproduce with credit granted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;Edward and Joanna Winn&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt; and their three children immigrated from England in 1639, first lived in Charlestown where in February 1640 he signed up to become one of the first Planters o
2 CONC f the newly planned town of Woburn. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;They soon moved to Woburn, where the first birth recorded in the Woburn Town Records was that of &lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;m
2 CONC y 8G Grandfather Increase Winn&lt;/strong&gt; on Dec, 5, 1641. Increase is the only known child born to Edward and Joanna in New England. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;Joanna died in Woburn on March 8, 1649. &lt;spa
2 CONC n style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;Nothing more is known of her. Edward remarried two more times.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family
2 CONC : 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;According to the Cleveland family genealogy, &amp;ldquo;It is conjectured that he [Edward Winn] was a master b
2 CONC uilder, and that he came from Ipswich, Eng., or vicinity, and that Moses Cleveland &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: #006aaa;"&gt;[My Note: who ma
2 CONC rried Edward&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: #006aaa;"&gt;and Joanna Winn&amp;rsquo;s daughter Ann, my 9G Aunt] &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;was his ap
2 CONC prentice and accompanied him thither. The first mention found of his [Edward Winn&amp;rsquo;s] name is when he appears at the house of Mr. Thomas Graves in Charlestown, as one of the Commissioners, at their first meeting, Dec. 18, 1640, held for co
2 CONC nsulting on the affairs of the contemplated town of Woburn.&amp;rdquo; &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;ldquo;Edward Winne&amp;rdquo; was one of the original 32 Planters of Woburn. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes
2 CONC ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;His name appears on the Feb. 8, 1640 list of subscribers along with Francis Wyman, Jr., his brother John Wyman, and their uncles (original spelling preserved) Ezekill, Samuwell, and Thomas Richison. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: #0070c0;"&gt;[My note: Francis Wyman Jr. was my 7G/8G grandfather based on two lines of descent.]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: non
2 CONC e;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Edward Winn became a Freeman on May 10, 1643, and he appears on the tax reco
2 CONC rds beginning in 1645. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;He held several offices, including: town officer, Surveyor for Sale 1658-9, and Selectman 1669. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;Ther
2 CONC e is a Winn Street in Woburn. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;His &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black; mso-themecolor: tex
2 CONC t1;"&gt;son Increase, my 8G Grandfather,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;is named in Edward&amp;rsquo;s will dated May 6, 1682.&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;Edward Winn died Sept. 5, 1682.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;Sources:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p
