2 CONC &lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt; was baptized on April 7, 1611 in Aller, Somerset, England, the same county where &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;Elisabetha Popley&amp;rdquo;,&lt;/strong&gt
2 CONC ; daughter of Guilielmi Popley, was baptized on Aug. 14, 1611. Per the authoritative FHL Film Number 1526638 Richardus Sawtell and Elizabetha Pople were&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman', serif; font-size: 12pt;"&
2 CONC gt;married in High Ham, Somerset, England on Feb. 5, 1627. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman', serif; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;They immigrated to Watertown, Massachusetts Bay Colony probably in 1630 since Richard Sawtell name i
2 CONC s engraved on the Founders Statue and Watertown was founded and settled in 1630. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman', serif; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman', serif
2 CONC ; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Accounts for Elizabeth&amp;rsquo;s date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;of birth and parentage v
2 CONC ary, but the above seems to be the most plausible based on the dates of birth of her children. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;Richard was listed as a Proprietor of Watertown in 1636/37 and their nine children were al
2 CONC l born there before 1655. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;Hannah my 8G Grandmother&lt;/strong&gt;, who married Sgt.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family
2 CONC : 'times new roman', serif; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Increase Winn was born in Watertown in 1642. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman', serif; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new ro
2 CONC man', serif; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;I found a copy of her handwritten birth record dated &amp;ldquo;10th of the 10th month 1642&amp;rdquo; [old calendar]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: non
2 CONC e;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Richard&amp;rsquo;s name is engraved on the Founders Statue in Watertown, left side, 16t
2 CONC h name down from the top.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;When Groton, Massachusetts Bay Colony, was first envisioned, Richard became a Proprietor and his family was among that town&amp;rsquo;s first settlers in 1655. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;A biography in the book &amp;ldquo;The Sawtell Family of Groton, MA&amp;rdquo; tells his story as follows:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'ti
2 CONC mes new roman',serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&amp;ldquo;Richard Sawtell, an early English Puritan, was a Proprietor of Watertown, Mass., in 1636-37 (sic); was one of the firs
2 CONC t Proprietors and original settlers of Groton, Mass., in 1655, and the town clerk for the first three years after its organization in 1662, 1663 and 1664. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;His children, according to Bond
2 CONC , were probably all born at Watertown, although only a few of their births are recorded there. The name, as is quite common with family names in the old records, is found at first variously spelled, Sawtel, Sautel, Sautle, Saretell and Sawtell, th
2 CONC e last form, however being the one generally used and adhered to by his Groton descendants. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;He had doubtless married in England prior to his departure for America. &lt;span style="mso-sp
2 CONC acerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;All that is known of his wife is that her name was Elizabeth, as shown in his will.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-
2 CONC family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&amp;ldquo;After the burning of Groton by the Indians in the spring of 1676, in King Philip&amp;rsquo;s War
2 CONC , Richard Sawtell returned to Watertown, where he died August 21, 1694. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;He had doubtless resumed his residence at Groton, though, if only for a few years prior to this, as his name appea
2 CONC rs among the heads of families living in the town in 1680, soon after its re-settlement. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;As an original Proprietor he had a twenty-acre right at Groton, his son, Jonathan, who died Jul
2 CONC y 6, 1690, a five acre right, and his son Zachariah a ten acre right. &lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;In Richard Sawtell&amp;rsquo;s will, dated May 16, 1692, on file, with others of the family, at the Probate Registr
2 CONC y for Middlesex county at East Cambridge, Mass., he mentions, as stated, his wife, Elizabeth who died October 18, 1694, and his nine children, only three of whom, Obadiah, Jonathan and Zachariah, established their homes at Groton and reared their f
2 CONC amilies there....&amp;rdquo;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Richard Sawtell&amp;rsquo;s will is handwritten and very hard to decipher. In it he names &lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;daughter Hannah Winn, my 8G Grandmother&lt;/strong&gt; as wel
2 CONC l as his wife Elizabeth and his other eight children as stated in the above biographical sketch.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt
2 CONC ;p style="mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'times new roman',serif; color: black;"&gt;Sources:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>
